    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p class=\"TableTitle\"><strong>Table&nbsp;10.XXX</strong> Frequent molecular alterations in uterine leiomyoma</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Karuna Garg",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"width: 700.7pt; border-collapse: collapse; mso-yfti-tbllook: 1184; mso-table-lspace: 9.0pt; margin-left: 6.75pt; mso-table-rspace: 9.0pt; margin-right: 6.75pt; mso-table-anchor-vertical: paragraph; mso-table-anchor-horizontal: column; mso-table-left: left; mso-padding-alt: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" border=\"0\" width=\"934\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" align=\"left\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 0; mso-yfti-firstrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 19.0pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 195.65pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 19.0pt;\" width=\"261\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Frequency</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 156.25pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 19.0pt;\" width=\"208\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Target(s)</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 192.45pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 19.0pt;\" width=\"257\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Mechanisms</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 156.35pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 19.0pt;\" width=\"208\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>References</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 1; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 57.8pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 195.65pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 57.8pt;\" width=\"261\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">70%</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 156.25pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 57.8pt;\" width=\"208\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><em>MED12</em></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">(Xq13.1)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 192.45pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 57.8pt;\" width=\"257\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Exon&nbsp;2 mutations</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 156.35pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 57.8pt;\" width=\"208\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{34626221; 22768200; 26037152; 27363490, 29666002; 27187686; 28592321; 21868628; 22428002}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 2; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 7.1pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 195.65pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"261\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">25&ndash;29%</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 156.25pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"208\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><em>HMGA2</em> (12q15)</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">and</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><em>HMGA1</em> (6q21)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 192.45pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"257\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Multiple fusion transcripts, commonly <em>HMGA2</em>::<em>RAD51B</em></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 156.35pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"208\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{30292626; 26787895; 28592321}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 3; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 7.1pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 195.65pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"261\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">4%</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 156.25pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"208\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><em>COL4A5</em> and <em>COL4A6</em> (Xq22)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 192.45pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"257\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Somatic or germline X-linked dominant Xq22 deletion (Alport syndrome&nbsp;/ diffuse leiomyomatosis)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 156.35pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"208\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{26787895; 28275241}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 4; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 7.1pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 195.65pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"261\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">1%</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 156.25pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"208\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><em>FH</em> (1q43)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 192.45pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"257\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Somatic (1q43 deletion, mutation, and biallelic inactivation) or germline autosomal dominant 1q43 mutation (hereditary leiomyomatosis and renal cell carcinoma syndrome)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 156.35pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"208\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{27363490; 11865300; 11248088; 39835370; 30741757; 16029320; 12772087; 26574848}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 5; mso-yfti-lastrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 7.1pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 195.65pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"261\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Unknown</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 156.25pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"208\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Epigenetic alterations (DNA methylation, histone modifications, chromatin remodelling)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 192.45pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"257\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Affects genes involved in tumour suppression, apoptosis, and extracellular matrix formation, contributing to leiomyoma growth</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 156.35pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 7.1pt;\" width=\"208\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{34349258; 35199155; 25922306}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 78098,
    "bookid": 85,
    "chapterId": 195,
    "fileId": 53457,
    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p class=\"TableTitle\"><strong>Box&nbsp;10.XXX</strong> Diagnostic criteria for leiomyoma morphological patterns</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Karuna Garg",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Usual-type leiomyoma</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Intersecting fascicles of bland spindle cells with eosinophilic cytoplasm and cigar-shaped nuclei lacking cytological atypia or tumour cell necrosis and with very low mitotic count</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Cellular leiomyoma</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">More cellular relative to surrounding myometrium; thick-walled blood vessels, clefted spaces; may have irregular border; cells have scant cytoplasm but lack cytological atypia; low mitotic count; no tumour cell necrosis</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Mitotically active leiomyoma</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Leiomyoma with &lt;&nbsp;8.42&nbsp;mitoses/mm<sup>2</sup> (6&ndash;19&nbsp;mitoses/10&nbsp;HPF of 0.55&nbsp;mm in diameter); no cytological atypia; no tumour cell necrosis</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Hydropic leiomyoma</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Leiomyoma with prominent oedematous stroma that leads to compartmentalization of the smooth muscle cells resulting in an epithelioid appearance</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Apoplectic leiomyoma</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">History of progesterone use or pregnancy; stellate zones of haemorrhage; zonation phenomenon; early infarct necrosis; may see mild to moderate cytological atypia and increased mitoses immediately adjacent to the necrosis but absent in areas distant from the necrosis</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Leiomyoma with bizarre nuclei</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Focal, multifocal, or diffuse bizarre cells; low mitotic count (&lt;&nbsp;2.1&nbsp;mitoses/mm<sup>2</sup>, typically &lt;&nbsp;5 mitoses/10&nbsp;HPF of 0.55&nbsp;mm in diameter); no cytological atypia; no tumour cell necrosis</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Lipoleiomyoma</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Admixture of mature adipocytes and smooth muscle cells</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Epithelioid leiomyoma</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Rounded or polygonal cells with eosinophilic granular or clear cytoplasm; no cytological atypia; no tumour cell necrosis; mitotic count &lt;&nbsp;0.8&nbsp;mitoses/mm<sup>2</sup> (equating to &lt;&nbsp;2&nbsp;mitoses/10&nbsp;HPF of 0.55&nbsp;mm in diameter)</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Myxoid leiomyoma</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Circumscribed, hypocellular, and myxoid; no cytological atypia or mitotic activity; no tumour cell necrosis; ALK negative</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Cotylenoid dissecting leiomyoma</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Irregular nodular dissection of bland smooth muscle cells within the myometrium</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Diffuse leiomyomatosis</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Innumerable, poorly circumscribed hypercellular tumour nodules within the myometrium; no cytological atypia or mitotic activity; no tumour cell necrosis</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Fumarate hydratase (FH)&ndash;deficient leiomyoma</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Staghorn blood vessels; alveolar oedema; chain-like arrangement of smooth muscle cells; eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions; prominent eosinophilic macronucleoli surrounded by clear perinucleolar haloes; these FH-deficient morphological features may be seen in usual-type leiomyoma, cellular leiomyoma, or leiomyoma with bizarre nuclei.</span></p>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  }
]